{
  "id": "counting-bits",
  "questionId": "counting-bits",
  "slug": "counting-bits",
  "title": "Counting Bits",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "computing each i's set-bit count from scratch repeats a lot of work - can popcount(i) be expressed in terms of popcount of some smaller number you've already computed?",
    "look at i in binary versus i >> 1 (i with its lowest bit dropped) - how do their set-bit counts relate?",
    "popcount(i) = popcount(i >> 1) + (i & 1) - the count for i is the count for i with the last bit chopped off, plus whether that last bit was a 1",
    "build the answer array bottom-up: dp[0] = 0, and for i from 1 to n, dp[i] = dp[i >> 1] + (i & 1)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "recomputing popcount for every i independently instead of reusing dp[i >> 1], turning an O(n) problem into O(n log n)",
    "using i >> 1 but forgetting to also add (i & 1) for the bit that got shifted off",
    "off-by-one on the array size - the result needs n + 1 entries, for i = 0 through n inclusive"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a non-negative integer `n`.\n\n**For every integer i from 0 to `n` (inclusive), compute the number of set bits in i's binary representation, and return the results as an array, ordered by i.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (result is just [0])",
      "n is a power of two",
      "n is one less than a power of two (e.g. 7), which has the most set bits for its size"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
